---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/urls';

const CONTACT = 'business@tacticalsolutions.com';

const navLinks = [
  { label: 'The pilot', href: '#pilot' },
  { label: 'Principles', href: '#principles' },
  { label: 'Price', href: '#price' },
  { label: 'Failure terms', href: '#failure' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Tactical Solutions Applied"
  description="Autonomy delivery for operators. One loop into production in 90 days, fixed fee, handed over with a runbook your team owns. No Loop Pilot has been run yet. Here are the terms before the first signature."
  canonicalPath="/applied"
  brandLabel="Tactical Solutions Applied"
  brandHref="/applied"
  navLinks={navLinks}
  navCta={{ label: 'Start a scoping call', href: '#apply' }}
  wide
>
  <section id="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Autonomy delivery</p>
    <h1>We hand you autonomy, not a PowerPoint.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Tactical Solutions Applied installs autonomous operations into your stack,
      on your data, and hands them over. One loop into production in 90 days for a
      fixed fee. Your team owns it at the end, with a runbook carrying your name
      rather than ours.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn" href="#apply">Start a scoping call</a>
      <a class="btn secondary" href="#pilot">What a pilot is</a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-proof">
      Pre-design-partner. We have not run a Loop Pilot yet, so none of this is a
      case study. It is the terms, published before the first signature. Two slots
      for Q1.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="pilot">
    <h2>The Loop Pilot.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      One operational loop, closed to production inside your stack, in 90 days,
      at a fixed price. Not a strategy deck. Not a proof of concept nobody runs.
      A working autonomous workflow handed over with the documentation to run it.
    </p>
    <p>
      "Loop" is the useful word here. It means a repeating piece of operational
      work with a decision inside it: a triage that has to be sorted, a
      non-conformance that has to be captured and dispositioned, a job that has to
      be scheduled against constraints. Pick the one eating the most hours from
      the fewest people. That is the pilot.
    </p>
    <p class="pull-line">The handoff is the deliverable.</p>
    <p>
      The demonstrable failure mode on autonomous systems is not the model. It is
      the handoff: the team that built it cannot let go, and the team receiving it
      cannot run it without the builders on a rope. So the handoff gets designed
      from day one, not bolted on at the end.
    </p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Weeks 1 to 2: scope.</h3>
        <p>
          The loop, the evidence it will run on, the boundaries of its autonomy
          inside your stack. Written down, because a scope that lives in a
          conversation cannot be tested at cutover.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Weeks 3 to 12: build, with the runbook running alongside.</h3>
        <p>
          The runbook is written in week one and rewritten every fortnight against
          what is actually happening. By cutover your team has edited parts of it
          themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Cutover.</h3>
        <p>
          The loop runs in production with your team on it and us beside them. Then
          we leave. Expansions are separate engagements, scoped with the knowledge
          that only exists once the first loop is running.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="aside-link">
      The three principles this runs on, argued in full:
      <a href={withBase('/pov/three-things-consulting-arm')}>three things we're designing our consulting arm around</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="principles">
    <h2>Three commitments, published before anyone signs.</h2>
    <p>
      Written down because once it is written down we have to hold it. If you are
      weighing us up, you should know the constraints before a call, not after.
    </p>
    <ul class="rule-list">
      <li>
        <strong>Handoff is the work, not the build.</strong> The runbook is a
        deliverable. Written week one, blunt, treating the operator as an adult.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One named human per loop. No committees.</strong> One person on
        your side with standing authority to pull the ripcord without asking
        anybody first. If a prospect cannot name them before signature, we do not
        sign. Distributed ownership at the start is an unowned system by month
        four.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One loop, fully shipped. Not three, half-shipped.</strong> The
        statement of work caps it at one loop into production. Overscoping at sale
        is how consulting delivery fails, and the three-loop pilot with two demos
        and a dead prototype is the standard shape.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="price">
    <h2>Fixed, and what moves it.</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>$150,000, fixed fee, 90 days, one loop, one measurable outcome.</strong>
      No day-rate creep, no change-order theatre, and a cutover date that does not
      drift because somebody found more scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first cohort is the only place that number moves. Take a slot before we
      have a named case study and we discount the Pilot for the right to publish
      it. You get the work cheaper, we get the reference. After that, the price is
      the price.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the number is wrong for your operation, there is a two-week paid scoping
      sprint at $25,000, credited against the Pilot if you go ahead. You keep the
      written scope either way. If the loop is not ready to run autonomously, we
      say so and you keep the document.
    </p>
    <p class="sub-line">
      Scope is agreed in writing before the fee is due. We quote nothing on a call
      that we will not put in a statement of work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="failure">
    <h2>What happens if it fails.</h2>
    <p>
      Every vendor's deck implies it will not. Worth naming, because it changes
      what you are actually buying.
    </p>
    <p>
      If we hit day 90 without a loop in production, you keep everything: the
      operator, the runbook, the audit trail, the scope. The fee is the fee and it
      does not come back, but nothing else does either. There is no licence to
      keep paying and no support contract that quietly becomes mandatory.
    </p>
    <p class="pull-line">
      The point of a handoff is that you do not need us afterwards. A consulting
      arm that needs to be needed has not done the job.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-for">
    <h2>Who this is for, and who it is not.</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>For operators with a real problem.</h3>
        <p>
          Industrial, logistics, field service, security, regulated operations. You
          have a loop eating hours your team cannot spare, a P&amp;L line that
          shows it, and someone who can own the outcome. You want it closed, not
          workshopped.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>For technical founders building vertical SaaS.</h3>
        <p>
          You need autonomy capability inside your product on a timeline you cannot
          hit with the team you have. The result has to survive you handing it to
          customers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Not for a transformation programme.</h3>
        <p>
          If the outcome you need is a roadmap, a maturity model and a steering
          group, a large firm does that better and we will say so. This is for a
          loop in production inside a quarter.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <dt>Has this run anywhere yet?</dt>
      <dd>
        Not yet. No Loop Pilot has been signed or run. The terms above are how the
        first one will work, published while the shape is still a decision rather
        than a story told after the fact.
      </dd>
      <dt>Is this the same product as ProSentinel or Weft?</dt>
      <dd>
        Same platform, same playbook, different delivery. Those two put autonomy
        into a product we run. Applied puts it into your stack, on your data, and
        hands it over. If you want a product rather than a build, start with
        <a href={withBase('/prosentinel')}>ProSentinel</a> or
        <a href={withBase('/weft')}>Weft PocketQS</a>.
      </dd>
      <dt>Who owns the code at the end?</dt>
      <dd>
        You do. The loop, the runbook and the audit trail run on your stack. There
        is no runtime licence attached to the pilot.
      </dd>
      <dt>What stops the system doing something stupid in production?</dt>
      <dd>
        Scope agreed in the first fortnight, actions bounded to what the loop
        actually owns, escalation designed in for the ambiguous cases. Nothing goes
        into production with an unbounded action, and the named human can stop it
        without asking.
      </dd>
      <dt>How much of my team's time does it take?</dt>
      <dd>
        More in weeks 1 to 2 than any week after it. Scoping needs the people who
        know the loop. After that it is the named human plus whoever the runbook
        names, and it drops away by cutover because that is the point.
      </dd>
      <dt>What does a scoping call cover?</dt>
      <dd>
        The loop, whether it is ready to run autonomously, who would own it, and
        the evidence it would run on. If the honest answer is that it is not ready,
        we say so on that call.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="apply">
    <h2>Two Loop Pilot slots for Q1.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      We need two things: the loop, and one name, the person who would own it. If
      you have the loop but not the name yet, send it anyway and we will work out
      whether that person exists.
    </p>
    <form id="intake" class="intake" novalidate>
      <p class="intake-intro">A scoping call starts here.</p>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-name">Name</label>
        <input id="f-name" name="name" type="text" autocomplete="name" required />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-role">Role</label>
        <input id="f-role" name="role" type="text" autocomplete="organization-title" required />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-company">Company</label>
        <input id="f-company" name="company" type="text" autocomplete="organization" required />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-loop">The loop you want closed</label>
        <textarea id="f-loop" name="loop" rows="3" required
          placeholder="What repeats, how often, and whose week it eats."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-owner">Who would own it on your side</label>
        <input id="f-owner" name="owner" type="text"
          placeholder="One name. Leave blank if nobody yet." />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="f-stack">What it would run on</label>
        <textarea id="f-stack" name="stack" rows="2"
          placeholder="Systems, data, and where a decision currently gets made."></textarea>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <button class="btn" type="submit">Send request</button>
        <span class="field-note">
          Opens your mail client, addressed to {CONTACT}, with these answers filled in.
        </span>
      </div>
    </form>
  </section>

  <section id="final-cta">
    <h2>One loop, one human, one handoff, 90 days.</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Fixed price, and the runbook is yours at the end whether it works or not.
    </p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn" href="#apply">Start a scoping call</a>
      <a class="btn secondary" href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>Email the team</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <div class="site-footer wide">
      <p class="footer-brand">Tactical Solutions · Applied</p>
      <nav class="footer-links" aria-label="Footer">
        <a href="#pilot">The pilot</a>
        <a href="#principles">Principles</a>
        <a href="#price">Price</a>
        <a href="#failure">Failure terms</a>
        <a href="#apply">Scoping call</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="small-print">
        Tactical Solutions Applied is the consulting arm of
        <a href={withBase('/')}>Tactical Solutions</a>, alongside
        <a href={withBase('/prosentinel')}>ProSentinel</a> and
        <a href={withBase('/weft')}>Weft PocketQS</a>. One autonomy layer, three
        go-to-markets.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero-proof {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .aside-link { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--fg-muted); }
  .sub-line { color: var(--fg-muted); max-width: 60ch; }
  .pull-line { font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .intake {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 4px;
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .intake-intro { margin: 0 0 1.25rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .field { margin-bottom: 1rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .field label { font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .field input,
  .field textarea {
    font: inherit;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .field input:focus,
  .field textarea:focus {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 1px;
  }
  .field-note { font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--fg-muted); }
  .faq dt { font-weight: 600; margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .faq dd { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; color: var(--fg-muted); max-width: 68ch; }
  .site-footer {
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem 2rem;
    max-width: 60rem;
  }
  .footer-brand { margin: 0 0 0.6rem; font-weight: 600; color: var(--fg); }
  .footer-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }
  .footer-links a { text-decoration: none; }
  .footer-links a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .small-print { margin: 0; max-width: 60ch; }
</style>

<script>
  // v0 submission path: compose a mailto with the field values prefilled.
  // No third-party form account and no spend. Swap to a form endpoint later
  // without changing the fields.
  const form = document.getElementById('intake') as HTMLFormElement | null;
  const CONTACT = 'business@tacticalsolutions.com';

  form?.addEventListener('submit', (event) => {
    event.preventDefault();
    const data = new FormData(form);
    const value = (key: string) => String(data.get(key) ?? '').trim();
    const lines = [
      `Name: ${value('name')}`,
      `Role: ${value('role')}`,
      `Company: ${value('company')}`,
      '',
      `Loop: ${value('loop')}`,
      `Owner on their side: ${value('owner') || 'not named yet'}`,
      '',
      `Would run on: ${value('stack')}`,
    ];
    const subject = `Loop Pilot scoping: ${value('company') || 'first cohort'}`;
    window.location.href =
      `mailto:${CONTACT}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(subject)}&body=${encodeURIComponent(lines.join('\n'))}`;
  });
</script>
